\begin_layout Standard
The relationship between inequality,
 motivation,
 and status-seeking behavior has generated extensive debate across both economics and sociology.
 While early economic thought often emphasized the functional role of inequality as a motivational force—
providing incentives for effort,
 investment,
 and innovation—
more recent perspectives have challenged this view,
 particularly in the context of constrained social mobility and stratified opportunity structures.
 Arguments in favor of inequality 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{BaronIncIneqMotivation2017}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 often emphasize efficiency and incentive compatibility 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{BeckerTomesEquilibMobility1979}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 based on the idea that greater rewards reflect greater effort.
 However,
 competing empirical and behavioral research suggests that beyond a certain threshold,
 inequality can undermine labor participation,
 erode social trust,
 and reduce long-term productivity 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{PerottiGrowthIncomeDis1993,AlesinaPerotti1996,StiglitzPriceofIneq2012}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 These contrasting perspectives remain visible in recent debates,
 reflecting an unresolved tension:
 whether inequality spurs economic participation or discourages it by limiting realistic pathways to advancement 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{ChettyLandofOpportunity2014}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang british
Inequality has also been closely tied to education policy,
 often motivating efforts to expand access to schooling.
 The conventional argument holds that greater access fosters mobility and mitigates disparities.
 Yet,
 critics argue that if education primarily serves as a signaling mechanism rather than a productivity enhancer,
 such expansion may lead to wasteful expenditure rather than genuine welfare improvement 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{AryalBullerLange2021,Durst2023,HungerfordSolon1987}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The tension underpins a continuing debate on whether inequality stimulates effort by rewarding mobility,
 or suppresses it by raising barriers and entrenching stagnation.
 In this context,
 participation costs emerge as a key determinant:
 high costs can neutralize the benefits of reduced inequality,
 whereas substantial income gaps may sustain participation despite unequal opportunities 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{HeckmanMosso2014}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang british
Status considerations further complicate this relationship.
 Although status or prestige has long been recognized as a key motivator of labour participation 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{BenabouTiroleIntrinsicExtrinsic2003,FrankPond}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
\lang english
the conventional view of status is one where it is contrasted as a social goal against the economic goals of a consumer – a dichotomy that complicates the endogenisation of status within economic models.
 
\lang british
Yet,
 status is neither independent of nor reducible to economic rewards
\lang english
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{goode1978celebrationheroes,moldovanuselacontestsstatus2007}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\lang british
.
 The sociological literature also emphasises this need to reconcile objective and subjective aspects of status 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{wegener1992prestigeconcepts}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 highlighting how both structural position and perceived rank shape behaviour.
 
\lang english
The competition-view of status that we adopt in the paper allows us to explicitly define the scope of the effects of income inequality by considering the motivations for objective status in a hierarchy of social positions.
 
\lang british
Within this framework,
 status influences economic outcomes indirectly:
 it does not enter utility directly through wealth,
 but through its effect on participation and relative ranking 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{postlewaite1998socialbasisinterdeppreferences}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang british
Perspectives in education literature also reflect these concerns – highlighting how education functions simultaneously as a productivity factor and a signal of underlying ability.
 Several approaches that highlight signaling or credntialist value of education contrast against the human capital theory – which views education primarily as skill acquisition.
 While Spence’s seminal framework 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{Spence1973}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 has established how credentials convey information,
 subsequent studies have detailed signaling effects across varied institutional contexts 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{Durst2023,Heisig}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The job-competition model from 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeA{Thurow1975}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 similarly describes how workers compete for positional advantages rather than wages,
 emphasizing relative position over absolute skill.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeA{Jencks1979}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 has also highlighted the dual economic and social character of social capital,
 which mediates how inequality interacts with long-term educational investments.
 
\lang english
More recently,
 
\lang british

\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeA{Gamoran2000}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 argue that educational success depends on both innate characteristics and social participation.
 Further research by 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeA{CamboniNiuPaiVohra2021}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 shows how institutional monitoring interacts with heterogeneous student types in outcomes.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang british
The present paper builds on this literature by integrating insights from signaling and social capital into a model where status is conceptualized as a dynamic position maintained through both ability and social capital investments - subject to income inequality.
 By endogenizing status,
 the model clarifies when inequality motivates participation and when it entrenches exclusion.
\end_layout

