\begin_layout Subsection
Basic Setup
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
noindent
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We consider a game where the winner strives to maintain a rich position solely to achieve higher economic gains.
 The two characteristics that matter in the competition where a poorer participant can be promoted to a rich position are i) skill - subject to the social capital investment from the participant and ii) raw-talent - that is unaffected by higher expenditures.
 Without loss of generality,
 both skill 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 (enhanced with expenditure) and the raw talent 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 (which cannot be changed with higher expenditure) are assumed to be uniformly distributed in the population.
 With a given weight 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 of raw-talent in the maximum total score considered for the win in a competition,
 the competitions are won with a highest score 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu r_{i}+(1-\mu)s_{i}$
\end_inset

 ( where 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 is raw-talent and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 are skills that are available with higher expenditure) which has a certain probability due to distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 We assume that the population remains constant over time (there are the same number of rich and poor in total) and that the participants are not aware of their 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 before participating in the competitions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is worth highlighting that in the above mechanism,
 a rich or a poor status by itself only has an indirect effect (through higher expenditure or budget) on the chance to be promoted (or maintaining one's rich position) - a probability that depends on the individual ability 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 and social-capital 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 All rich participants and all poor participants have the same preferences so that the individual attributes 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 - which are not known to the participant - are not used to decide upon the expenditure (effort) towards the status game.
 With 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 uniformly distributed,
 the likelihood of any participant 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in[1,N]$
\end_inset

 can be formulated as the probability for their total score 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu r_{i}+(1-\mu)s_{i}$
\end_inset

 being higher than any other participant - where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 is a parameter that combines social-capital 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and raw-talent 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 so that the participant with maximum score 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu r+(1-\mu)s$
\end_inset

 wins the competition.
 The winner of the competition acquires a rich position with economic gains so that she obtains the disposable income 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 while the loser acquires the poor position receive 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}<y_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To start with a simpler case,
 consider that in a competition with raw-talents alone,
 the winning participant is one who has the maximum 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 drawn from the sample 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

.
 In other words,
 the winner in a competition of raw-talents is a player 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\{r_{i}<r_{j}\forall i\in[1,N]\}$
\end_inset

 and the probability that a given participant 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 of raw-skill 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the winner is essentially that of all other players 
\begin_inset Formula $j\ne i$
\end_inset

 having ability 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{j\ne i}$
\end_inset

 less than 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 This is the probability 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\int_{0}^{1}{P(r_{i}>r_{1})\times P(r_{i}>r_{2})\times...P(r_{i}>r_{i-1})\times P(r_{i}>r_{i+1})...P(r>r_{N})}dr_{i}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With a uniform distribution of skills 
\begin_inset Formula $r\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $P(r_{i}\ge r)=r_{i}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in[1,N]$
\end_inset

 and therefore,
 the probability is evaluates to 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{0}^{1}{(r_{i})^{N-1}}dr_{i}=\frac{1}{N}$
\end_inset

.
 With more players,
 the chances of a particular participant winning evidently declines.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now introducing the skills 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 consider a threshold investment 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 which is necessary to be spent for the social capital to be available to a participant.
 While this is an arrangement that evidently favours the rich participant,
 there are also diminishing returns to investing 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 towards the richer position if naturally occurring raw talent 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 is also significant in the competition.
 Focusing on cases where signaling value is significant,
 we assume that social capital is more important than raw-talent.
 The foregoing analysis thus assumes that that 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\mu>\mu$
\end_inset

 – a condition that has been used in solutions of the model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "assu:mu_less_than_half"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\mu<\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
noindent
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the threshold investment 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

,
 the participants face different probabilities to win depending on whether they spend 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 or not.
 More specifically,
 the participant takes advantage of the skills with weight 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\mu$
\end_inset

 only when she spends an amount 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

.
 The participant might avoid the investment altogether and keep the investment towards savings instead.
 The behaviour of all participants depends only on 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{1},\nu_{2}$
\end_inset

 - as they are unaware of 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i},s_{i}$
\end_inset

 before they participate.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The result depends on the following distribution function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\equiv\mu r_{i}+(1-\mu)s_{i}$
\end_inset

 under the Assumption 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "assu:mu_less_than_half"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 are independent random variables uniformly distributed between 0 and 1 (see Appendix 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
ref{sec:appendix_theorertical_1}
\end_layout

\end_inset

) .
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
F_{X}(x) & =P(x_{k}<x)=P(r_{k}\mu+s_{k}(1-\mu)<x)=\begin{cases}
\frac{x^{2}}{2\mu(1-\mu)} & 0<x<\mu\\
\frac{x-\mu/2}{1-\mu} & \mu<x<1-\mu\\
1-\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2\mu(1-\mu)} & 1-\mu<x<1\\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the game where a participant 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 with the maximum score 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the winner,
 the number of rich positions is fixed to 
\begin_inset Formula $N-P$
\end_inset

 so that only the top-scorer gets to occupy the only rich position available to be filled in every competition.
 The behaviour of the rich and the poor participants in the long-term can thus be explained with four different cases.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Case I - The poor participant does not participate due to insufficient investment 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{1}=0$
\end_inset

 while the rich participant does participate by spending 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{2}=\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 .
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Case II - The rich participant does not participate with 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{2}=0$
\end_inset

 but the poor participant does participate i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{1}=\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Case III - Both poor and rich participants do not participate so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{1}=\nu_{2}=0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Case IV - Both poor rich and participant participate with 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{1}=\nu_{2}=\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
noindent
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With Case I,
 the poor participant can win when her raw-talent (weighted with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

) is higher than the total score of all other poor participants as well as the total score of the rich participants (who compete with acquired skills).
 The poor are priced out of accessing any skills 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 in their score in Case I so that only the rich participants with investment 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{2}=$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 have skills 
\begin_inset Formula $s>0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 a given poor participant must be ahead both of all of the poor participants and the rich participants so that a participant 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 may win only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu r_{i}$
\end_inset

 is higher than than raw-talent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu r_{k}$
\end_inset

 in all poor participants and also exceeds the score 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu r_{k}+(1-\mu)s_{k}$
\end_inset

 involving skills among the rich participants
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
P_{1}(\text{win}|r_{i}) & =\prod_{k=1,k\ne i}^{P}P(\mu r_{k}<\mu r_{i})\prod_{k=P+1}^{N}P(\mu r_{k}+(1-\mu)s_{k}<\mu r_{i})=r_{i}^{P-1}F_{X}(\mu r_{i})^{N-P}\label{eq:CaseI_general}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore,
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
P_{1}(\text{win}|r_{i}) & =\begin{cases}
\frac{\mu^{N-P}r_{i}^{2N-P-1}}{2\mu(1-\mu)} & 0<r_{i}<\mu\\
r_{i}^{P-1}(\frac{\mu r_{i}-\mu/2}{1-\mu})^{N-P} & \mu<r_{i}<1-\mu\\
r_{i}^{P-1}(1-\frac{(\mu r_{i}-1)^{2}}{2\mu(1-\mu)})^{N-P} & 1-\mu<r_{i}<1\\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the uniform density for 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 (i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $f(r_{i})=1$
\end_inset

 ),
 total probability to win can be calculated as
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{r_{i}=0}^{r_{i}=1}P(\text{win}|r_{i})dr_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Since the participants do not know the values of 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 before they participate in such competitions,
 the total probability can be evaluated with the assumption that both 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 range from 0 to 1.
 Notice also that since only one participant can win the skills-game,
 the probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{0}^{1}\int_{0}^{1}P_{2}(\text{win}|r_{i},s_{i})ds_{i}dr_{i}$
\end_inset

 for a rich participant to win is simply 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{N-P}-\frac{P}{N-P}\int_{0}^{1}\int_{0}^{1}P_{1}(\text{win}|r_{i},s_{i})ds_{i}dr_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Case II,
 when the rich person has no access to social capital (i.e.
 all rich participants have 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{2}=0$
\end_inset

),
 the poor participant has the skills-advantage in the game so the probability to win for the poor participant 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{multline}
P(\text{win}|r_{i},s_{i})=\prod_{k=1,k\ne i}^{P}P(\mu r_{k}+(1-\mu)s_{k}<\mu r_{i}+(1-\mu)s_{i})\prod_{k=P+1}^{N}P(\mu r_{k}<\mu r_{i}+(1-\mu)s_{i})\\
=F_{X}(x_{i})^{P-1}\prod_{k=P+1}^{N}P(\mu r_{k}<\mu r_{i}+(1-\mu)s_{i})\label{eq:CaseII_general}
\end{multline}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Case III,
 when neither the rich nor the poor participate in the game,
 then the game depends only on raw-talents and all participants have the same probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{N}$
\end_inset

 to win the game.
 This is because 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{r_{i}=0}^{r_{i}=1}P(\text{win}|r_{i})dr_{i}=\frac{r_{i}^{N}}{N}|_{0}^{1}=\frac{1}{N}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $P(\text{win}|r_{i})=\prod_{k=1,k\ne i}^{P}P(r_{k}<r_{i})\prod_{k=P+1}^{N}P(r_{k}<r_{i})=r_{i}{}^{N-1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Case IV,
 when both rich and poor participants set 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{1}=\nu_{2}=\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

,
 then the poor participant can win only if her score 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is above all other participants - rich or poor.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{multline}
P(\text{win}|r_{i})=\prod_{k=1,k\ne i}^{P}P(\mu r_{k}+(1-\mu)s_{k}<\mu r_{i}+(1-\mu)s_{i})\prod_{k=P+1}^{N}P(\mu r_{k}+(1-\mu)s_{k}<\mu r_{i}+(1-\mu)s_{i})\\
=F_{X}(x_{i})^{N-1}\label{eq:CaseIV_general}
\end{multline}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using symmetry arguments,
 this winning probability - which is the same for all participants - also turns out to be 
\begin_inset Formula $1/N$
\end_inset

 for all the participants.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the rich and poor participants having disparate incomes 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 (so that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}<y_{2}$
\end_inset

),
 the above setup evidently puts the poor participants at a disadvantage since they may not afford to pay the costs to enhance their skills (i.e.
 realise the true worth of their skills 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 due to high participation costs).
 However,
 since both raw-talent 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 and social-capital 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 (the latter enhanced by costly institutional support) are randomly distributed in the population,
 the competition may never rule out the chance for a poor participant to win.
 For the same reason,
 the returns from investments to enhance one's skills may also be diminishing since one cannot beat raw-talent and non-enhanced skills after a certain point.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Two-player-Discrete-Model"

\end_inset

Equilibrium Conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
noindent
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The solution follows by setting total number of participants 
\begin_inset Formula $N=2$
\end_inset

 and the number of poor participants 
\begin_inset Formula $P=1$
\end_inset

 in Equations 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:CaseI_general"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:CaseII_general"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:CaseIV_general"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 above.
 As before,
 a participant takes advantage of the skills with weight 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\mu$
\end_inset

 only by spending an amount 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 and can decide to avoid the expenditure altogether in order to invest it towards the savings instead.
 It is easy to show that these results do not change for 
\begin_inset Formula $P>\frac{N}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 We first calculate the probability to win in Cases I,
 II,
 III and IV and then discuss the equilibrium conditions using an intertemporal additive utility function.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Winning Probability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Case I
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Case I,
 the poor participant can win when her raw-talent 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{1}$
\end_inset

 is higher than of the total score of rich participant (who competes with acquired skills).
 The poor participant is priced out of accessing any skills 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 in her score so that the skills 
\begin_inset Formula $s>0$
\end_inset

 is only true for the rich participant having 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{2}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $=\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 the poor participant with raw-talent 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{1}$
\end_inset

 can win with the following probability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
P(\text{win}|r_{1}) & =P(\mu r_{2}+(1-\mu)s_{2}<\mu r_{1})=F_{X}(\mu r_{1})
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Notice that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu r_{1}<\mu<1-\mu<1$
\end_inset

 is implied due to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu<\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 (and 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{1}\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 ).
 The probability that the total score 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu r_{2}+(1-\mu)s_{2}$
\end_inset

 does not exceed 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu r_{1}$
\end_inset

 implies that only the first part of the piece-wise function for 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{X}(x)$
\end_inset

 needs to be considered.
 Therefore,
 using 
\begin_inset Formula $P(\text{win}|r_{1})=\frac{\mu^{2}r_{1}^{2}}{2\mu(1-\mu)}$
\end_inset

,
 we evaluate 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{1}(\text{win})$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
P_{1}(\text{win})=\int_{0}^{1}P(\text{win}|r_{1})dr_{1} & =\frac{\mu}{6(1-\mu)}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

%integrate (mu^2*r^2,r,0,1)/(2*mu*(1-mu)) gives 
\backslash
frac{
\backslash
mu}{6(1-
\backslash
mu)}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

% for .2,
 the probability is .03125.
 The MC gives .03126 .
 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since only one of the participants can win,
 the probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{2}(\text{win})$
\end_inset

 for the rich participant to win is simply 
\begin_inset Formula $1-P_{1}(\text{win})$
\end_inset

.
 The following thus applies to all other cases (i.e.
 Cases II,
 III and IV).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\xout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
P_{2}(\text{win}) & =1-P_{1}(\text{win})\label{eq:two_player_rich_probability}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Case II
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Case II,
 the rich person does not participate (
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{2}=0$
\end_inset

) and both the poor participants have the skills-advantage in the game.
 A poor participant wins by having the total score higher than the raw-talent of the rich participant (who does not acquire skills with investment).
 Representing 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\equiv\mu r_{i}+(1-\mu)s_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 we have the following probability for the poor participant to win
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
P(\text{win}|r_{1},s_{1}) & =P(\mu r_{2}<\mu r_{1}+(1-\mu)s_{1})
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Notice that 
\begin_inset Formula $P(\mu r_{2}<\mu r_{1}+(1-\mu)s_{1})$
\end_inset

 (i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $P(r_{2}<r_{1}+(\frac{1}{\mu}-1)s_{1})$
\end_inset

 ) is 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 after 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{1}+(\frac{1}{\mu}-1)s_{1}>1$
\end_inset

 ( or 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu r_{1}+(1-\mu)s_{1}>\mu)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\mu}$
\end_inset

 in the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $(0,\mu)$
\end_inset

 (this is because 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{2}\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 ).
 In other words,
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
P(r_{2}<r_{1}+(1/\mu-1)s_{1}) & =\begin{cases}
\frac{x_{1}}{\mu} & 0<x_{1}<\mu\\
1 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The probability to win for the poor participant can be written as (see Appendix 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
ref{sec:appendix_theorertical_1}
\end_layout

\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
P(\text{win}|r_{1},s_{1}) & =\begin{cases}
\frac{x_{1}}{\mu} & 0<x<\mu\\
1 & \mu<x_{1}<1-\mu\\
1 & 1-\mu<x_{1}<1\\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using double-integral properties (see Appendix 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
ref{sec:appendix_theorertical_2}
\end_layout

\end_inset

),
 the total probability to win 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{1}(\text{win})$
\end_inset

 for a poor participant is evaluated as follows
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{multline*}
P_{1}(\text{win})=\int_{s_{1}=0}^{s_{1}=1}\int_{r_{1}=0}^{r_{1}=1}P(\text{win}|r_{1},s_{1})ds_{1}dr_{1}=\int_{r_{1}=0}^{r_{1}=1}{\int_{s_{1}=0}^{s_{1}=\frac{\mu(1-r_{1})}{1-\mu}}\ \frac{(\mu r_{1}+(1-\mu)s_{1})}{\mu}ds_{1}dr_{1}}\\
+\int_{r_{1}=0}^{r_{1}=1}{\int_{s_{1}=\frac{\mu(1-r_{1})}{1-\mu}}^{s_{1}=1-\frac{\mu r_{1}}{1-\mu}}1\ ds_{1}dr_{1}}\\
+\int_{r_{1}=0}^{r_{1}=1}{\int_{s_{1}=1-\frac{\mu r_{1}}{1-\mu}}^{s_{1}=1}1\ ds_{1}dr_{1}}\\
=\frac{\mu}{3(1-\mu)}+\frac{1-2\mu}{1-\mu}+\frac{\mu}{2(1-\mu)}\\
=\frac{2\mu}{6(1-\mu)}+\frac{6(1-2\mu)}{6(1-\mu)}+\frac{3\mu}{6(1-\mu)}=\frac{6-7\mu}{6(1-\mu)}
\end{multline*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{2}(\text{win})$
\end_inset

 for the rich participant to win follows from Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:two_player_rich_probability"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Case III 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Case III,
 when neither the rich nor the poor participate in the game,
 then the game depends only on raw-talents and both participants have the same probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 to win the game.
 This is because 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{r_{i}=0}^{r_{i}=1}P(\text{win}|r_{i})dr_{i}=\frac{r_{i}^{2}}{2}|_{0}^{1}=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Both the poor and rich participant have the same total probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 to win.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Case IV
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Case IV,
 when both rich and poor participants set 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{1}=\nu_{2}=\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

,
 then the poor participant can win only if her score 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is above that of the rich participant.
 Therefore,
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{multline*}
P(\text{win}|r_{1},s_{1})=P(\mu r_{2}+(1-\mu)s_{2}<\mu r_{1}+(1-\mu)s_{1})\\
=F_{X}(x_{1})
\end{multline*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
P(\text{win}|r_{1},s_{1}) & =\begin{cases}
\frac{x_{1}^{2}}{2\mu(1-\mu)} & 0<x_{1}<\mu\\
\frac{x_{1}-\mu/2}{1-\mu} & \mu<x_{1}<1-\mu\\
1-\frac{(x_{1}-1)^{2}}{2\mu(1-\mu)} & 1-\mu<x_{1}<1\\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While this integral can be evaluated based on symmetry arguments ( since 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is also randomly distributed),
 the integral 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{s_{1}=0}^{s_{1}=1}\int_{r_{1}=0}^{r_{1}=1}P(\text{win}|r_{1},s_{1})dr_{1}ds_{1}$
\end_inset

 also evaluates to 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 (see Appendix 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
ref{sec:appendix_theorertical_2}
\end_layout

\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{multline*}
\int_{s=0}^{s=1}\int_{r=0}^{r=1}F_{X}(x_{1})ds_{1}dr_{1}=\int_{r_{1}=0}^{r_{1}=1}{\int_{s_{1}=0}^{s_{1}=\frac{\mu(1-r)}{1-\mu}}\frac{(\mu r_{1}+(1-\mu)s_{1})^{2}}{2\mu(1-\mu)}\ ds_{1}dr_{1}}\\
+\int_{r_{1}=0}^{r_{1}=1}{\int_{s_{1}=\frac{\mu(1-r)}{1-\mu}}^{s_{1}=1-\frac{\mu r}{1-\mu}}(\frac{\mu r_{1}+(1-\mu)s_{1}-\mu/2}{1-\mu})\ ds_{1}dr_{1}}\\
+\int_{r_{1}=0}^{r_{1}=1}{\int_{s_{1}=1-\frac{\mu r}{1-\mu}}^{s_{1}=1}(1-\frac{(\mu r_{1}+(1-\mu)s_{1}-1)^{2}}{2\mu(1-\mu)})\ ds_{1}dr_{1}}\\
=\frac{\mu^{2}}{8(1-\mu)^{2}}+\frac{1-2\mu}{2(1-\mu)}+\frac{(4-5\mu)\mu}{8(1-\mu)^{2}}=\frac{4(1-\mu)^{2}}{8(1-\mu)^{2}}=\frac{1}{2}
\end{multline*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

